d-4fae3beb-3510-5939-afed-b54d6ee4d3a2" :answer-field/choices "answer-9c99d273-eee7-551e-991e-7d2f5b565510"] ["question-09efb2e6-1e80-53f4-bf82-55db52baf7d8" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-4fae3beb-3510-5939-afed-b54d6ee4d3a2"] [17592186083338 :knowledge-point/questions "question-09efb2e6-1e80-53f4-bf82-55db52baf7d8"] ["question-2e20c6e9-8a1f-5520-84a5-13e0b1715760" :question/id #uuid "2e20c6e9-8a1f-5520-84a5-13e0b1715760"] ["question-2e20c6e9-8a1f-5520-84a5-13e0b1715760" :question/math-academy-id "q-134126"] ["question-2e20c6e9-8a1f-5520-84a5-13e0b1715760" :question/problem "![](images/27/271e5f80ff6ab311bcdbc6b2725516b16a5c3fee63b6d219a59801c78d4f77e4.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the number of shirts and the number of pants sold in a store over a certain number of days. \nWhich of the following is the best approximation of the correlation coefficient?"] ["question-2e20c6e9-8a1f-5520-84a5-13e0b1715760" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\rho \\le 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho =1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho =-1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nFrom the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear correlation.\n\nTherefore, the best approximation of the correlation coefficient must be $\\rho ≈0.$"] ["question-2e20c6e9-8a1f-5520-84a5-13e0b1715760" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-157cc70f-8bfd-56d9-b601-f1ea6f2f9206" :answer-field/id #uuid "157cc70f-8bfd-56d9-b601-f1ea6f2f9206"] ["answer-e340584d-ae6e-5e84-a152-3f6a2121b262" :answer/id #uuid "e340584d-ae6e-5e84-a152-3f6a2121b262"] ["answer-e340584d-ae6e-5e84-a152-3f6a2121b262" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e340584d-ae6e-5e84-a152-3f6a2121b262" :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.5"] ["answer-b3b980a5-1b07-53a0-bd99-f5969d3971f0" :answer/id #uuid "b3b980a5-1b07-53a0-bd99-f5969d3971f0"